<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Will Play the Drums in the Parade. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Talents Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Will Play the Drums in the Parade.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Recognize the main ideas of the conversation when interacting in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify expressions of feelings and attitudes about participating in Fiestas Patrias celebrations (e.g., 'I'm proud to march in the parade.' 'I feel nervous about dancing in front of the whole school.').
            Can recognize main ideas during group discussions about Fiestas Patrias preparations (e.g., 'Who will carry the Panamanian flag?' 'I will play the drums during the parade.').
            Can understand specific details in conversations about parade roles and tasks (e.g., 'You need to wear your traditional uniform.' 'Don't forget to bring your instrument for the parade.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of Fiestas Patrias parades, different musical instruments (drums, accordion, flute), and students marching. The teacher plays a short audio clip of parade music. Students listen and raise their hands if they recognize the sounds. The teacher asks, "What do you hear?" and "What do you see?" to elicit initial ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary for the lesson using flashcards or realia: 'parade', 'drums', 'flute', 'accordion', 'band', 'march', 'perform', 'excited', 'nervous', 'proud'. The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of each word, focusing on short vowels /ʌ/ in 'fun' (e.g., 'drum') and /æ/ in 'band'. Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models short sentences using the target grammar: "I <b>will play</b> the drums." "I <b>can play</b> the flute." "If I <b>practice</b>, I <b>will perform</b> well."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'parade' quiet or noisy?" (Expected answer: Noisy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can everyone play the 'drums'?" (Expected answer: No, only if you learn/practice.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you are 'excited', are you happy or sad?" (Expected answer: Happy!)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Talents Around Us".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two 6th-grade students, María and José, discussing their roles and feelings about participating in the upcoming Fiestas Patrias parade. The dialogue incorporates the target grammar (future simple, first conditional, modal 'can') and vocabulary. For example: "I will play the drums in the band." "I feel nervous about dancing in front of the whole school." "If I practice my flute every day, I will perform beautifully." "Can you help me with my costume?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand main ideas and specific details in short, clear audio conversations about familiar topics related to school events and personal talents.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and then answer a general question: "What are María and José talking about?" (Expected answer: The school parade/Fiestas Patrias). "How do they feel about it?" (Expected answer: Excited, nervous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen and complete a True/False exercise based on specific details mentioned in the dialogue. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">María will play the accordion. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">José feels nervous about dancing. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They need to practice their parts. (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for the future simple tense for plans, the first conditional for possibilities, and the modal 'can' for ability, along with target vocabulary (parade, drums, flute, band, march, perform, excited, nervous, proud, costume, practice).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences from the dialogue, but with key words or phrases (related to target grammar or vocabulary) missing. Students listen to short excerpts of the audio dialogue again (played 2-3 times) and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I _______ play the drums." (will)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I _______ every day, I _______ beautifully." (practice, will perform)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I _______ play the flute." (can)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel _______ about the _______." (excited, parade)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher then checks answers with the class, playing parts of the audio again for verification.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Match/Categorize):</b> The teacher plays short, distinct sentences or phrases from the dialogue. Students listen and match them to categories on their worksheet: "Plans for the parade" (e.g., "I will march with the band."), "Abilities" (e.g., "I can play the guitar."), or "Feelings/Attitudes" (e.g., "I'm proud to carry the flag."). Students also listen for rhyming words like 'drum' and 'hum' versus non-rhyming words like 'flag' and 'march', ticking a box for rhyming pairs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays a series of short statements related to parade preparations (e.g., "I will carry the banner.", "If it rains, we will stay inside."). One student listens and repeats the sentence, while the other student has a list of possible actions/feelings and confirms if their partner heard correctly and understood the main idea or specific detail (e.g., "Yes, that's a plan for the parade."). They listen to each other's pronunciation of minimal pairs in words like 'band' and 'bend' (if applicable from other vocabulary) or distinguish rhyming words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Talents Around Us".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Parade Planning Radio Show" - The teacher plays a simulated short "radio show" segment (approx. 2-3 minutes) featuring interviews with three different students from another school, talking about their roles, talents, and feelings for their Fiestas Patrias parade. Each student mentions their instrument, their role, and how they feel, using 'will', 'can', and 'if... will'. Students will listen to identify specific details and main ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "I Will Play the Drums in the Parade." and Scenario: "The Talents Around Us".</b> The audio focuses on different students showcasing their talents for the parade and expressing their plans and feelings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the "Parade Planning Radio Show" and explains the task: Students need to listen carefully and complete a "Parade Roles Chart" based on the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The chart has columns for "Student Name," "Instrument/Role," and "Feeling/Attitude."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio once for gist. Students try to fill in as much as they can.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students complete the chart, listening for main ideas ("What will Carlos do?") and specific details ("What instrument will he play? How does he feel?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups, students compare their charts and discuss what they heard, using phrases like "Did you hear that Sara will play the accordion?" or "I think Juan feels excited." This allows for peer confirmation of listening comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and a short listening comprehension quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a new, short audio announcement (1 minute) about a change in parade plans or an additional role. Students listen and answer 3-4 multiple-choice or short-answer questions to demonstrate comprehension of main ideas, specific details, and feelings/attitudes. For example: "What is the main news about the parade?" "Who will now carry the flag?" "How does the announcement presenter sound?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main ideas from the audio (e.g., 2/2 correct).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying specific details (e.g., 2/3 correct).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to recognize expressions of feelings/attitudes (e.g., 'excited', 'proud', 'nervous').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and collaboration during group tasks (observed).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new words you understood today about Fiestas Patrias or talents."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one main idea you heard in the last audio announcement?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you find easy or difficult about listening to English conversations today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can practicing listening help you understand your friends when they talk about their plans?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening strategies worked well today? Were the audio inputs at the appropriate CEFR A2.1 level for 6th grade?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully identify main ideas and specific details? What adjustments or scaffolds (e.g., visual aids, pre-teaching more vocabulary, slower audio speed) might be needed for students who struggled?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage students to recognize expressions of feelings and attitudes in the next listening tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or story related to Fiestas Patrias and different talents, using many of the same vocabulary words and grammatical structures introduced today to build comprehension across skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>